---
machine:
  type: controlplane
  nodeLabels:
    topology.kubernetes.io/zone: dmf-u
  install:
    diskSelector:
      model: Samsung SSD 870
    # 2026-07-25: RTX 3090 arrived, tested in the AOOSTAR AG02 dock (ASMedia ASM2464
    # bridge) - never stable, hardware-level flakiness on that dock's bridge chip.
    # 2026-07-28: swapped to a PELADN Link S-3 dock (Intel Barlow Ridge JHL9480 TB5
    # controller) - link itself rock solid, zero disconnects. Two eGPU-schematic
    # attempts that day (open driver, then proprietary driver) both reproduced the
    # same hang: `ext-nvidia-persistenced` blocks boot on /sys/bus/pci/drivers/nvidia
    # existing, and machine.kernel.modules either infinite-retries (ENODEV, dock not
    # yet connected) or the node hard-hangs minutes after a successful load. Reverted
    # to true baseline both times, caught by Talos's own hardware watchdog (iTCO_wdt,
    # 5m timeout) when left unattended. Filed as siderolabs/talos discussion #13896.
    # NOTE: an earlier version of this comment claimed a machine.udev.rules hotplug
    # trigger was "the correct fix" - that was this session's own unverified
    # speculation, not anything from Talos documentation, and shouldn't have been
    # written up as settled guidance. No such requirement appears anywhere in the
    # official NVIDIA-on-Talos docs.
    # 2026-07-29: two confounds from the earlier attempts addressed before retrying -
    # (1) the dock's USB Realtek NIC (driver r8152) is now blacklisted, eliminating a
    # possible source of boot-time instability unrelated to the GPU itself; (2) GPU
    # Operator now installed underneath kube-system/nvidia-gpu-resource-driver per
    # the documented pattern (discussion #13896). Retried with the OSS driver
    # (nvidia-open-gpu-kernel-modules-production) - hard-locked again (network
    # connectivity lost) on both a warm kexec reboot with the dock pre-connected
    # AND a genuine full cold boot (power off, dock connected, power on), r8152
    # still blacklisted either way. Confounds addressed made no difference.
    # Switching to the proprietary driver (nonfree-kmod-nvidia) with the exact
    # same machine.kernel.modules block, to check whether driver flavor matters
    # under the cold-boot condition specifically (the earlier proprietary attempt
    # predated the cold-boot test and used a warm kexec reboot instead).
    image: factory.talos.dev/installer/382808c804bdc4c1a33f4064373ec7115c753bc9aced28dedba12390cd78bc9c:v1.13.7 # eGPU schematic (proprietary driver) - see `just talos gen-schematic-id-egpu-proprietary`
    wipe: false
  kernel:
    modules:
      - name: nvidia
      - name: nvidia_uvm
      - name: nvidia_drm
      - name: nvidia_modeset
---
apiVersion: v1alpha1
kind: HostnameConfig
hostname: k8s-2
